Split the new-session repository picker into a Repositories tab and a Custom
tab, list the repositories configured in Settings on any host, and let admins
give a custom repository its own Git token or SSH key.

The repository popover keeps the picked repositories on top. Below them, the
Repositories tab has one combobox with two groups: the connected GitHub
account's repositories, and the repositories configured in Settings ->
Repository variables (GET /v1/repo-env/repositories, any host, fetched once
the popover first opens). A configured repository the GitHub list already has
appears there only, with its branches; the others get a typed branch. The tab
says when the GitHub list is loading, failed to load or GitHub isn't
connected. The Custom tab (components/RepoCustomForm.tsx) takes any HTTPS or
git@ URL and a branch. Without the GitHub App and configured repositories
there are no tabs, only the Custom form.

For admins, the Custom form also picks a credential: none, a Git token or an
SSH key. Choosing one saves it on the repository with
PUT /v1/repo-env/credentials (images/server/omnigent_repo_env_api.py) before
adding the repository, keeping its other variables. The form refuses a token
for an SSH URL and a key for an HTTPS URL, and says when a new one replaces
the stored one. Non-admins see that an admin can add one.

Settings -> Repository variables gets an SSH key switch beside the Git token
switch, with a write-only field: blank keeps the stored key, and switching it
off removes it. The card lists "SSH key".

The icons next to text in the popover and on the Settings card sit in a
.cap-icon holder (index.css), which centres them on the text's capitals in any
font, instead of 1-2px below them.Base: omnigent v0.18.0 (a8676d23e88d).

